This section gives prefill of {{ n(I.prefill.new_tokens) }} new tokens on top of a cached {{ n(I.prefill.context) }}-token prefix. Each platform is at the
number of prompts in flight that makes its prefill cheapest (we tried 1, 4, 16 and 48). A pipelined design
such as S needs several prompts in flight to use all its layers. With one prompt, all but one of its
{{ I.designs.s.area.tiles }} tiles are idle.

| platform | prompts in flight | one prompt alone | prefill tok/s | $/M prefill tokens | one prompt alone: $/M |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
{% for k in ["g0_x4", "g0_x8", "g1_x4", "a_x4", "s", "f"] %}
| {{ I.prefill.rows[k].label }} | {{ I.prefill.rows[k].prompts_in_flight }} | {{ n(I.prefill.rows[k].ms_one_prompt, 0) }} ms | {{ n(I.prefill.rows[k].tok_s, -2) }} | {{ usd(I.prefill.rows[k].usd_per_mtok_prefill, 4) }} | {{ usd(I.prefill.rows[k].usd_per_mtok_prefill_one_prompt, 4) }} |
{% endfor %}

Prefill is where the gain of S is smallest. At its best concurrency S prefills at {{ x(I.prefill.s_over_g0_cost, 2) }} the GPUs' cost per
token. The HBM chip is the cheapest at prefill ({{ x(I.prefill.a_over_g0_cost, 1) }} the GPUs). Prefill processes many tokens in parallel,
and GPUs and HBM chips do that well. The engines publish an agentic workload with
{{ n(I.prefill.prefill_tokens_per_output_token, 0) }} new prompt tokens per output token after cache hits. On that workload, GPU prefill adds about
{{ usd(I.prefill.gpu_pool_adder_usd_per_mtok_output, 2) }} per million output tokens.

Our first version ran the prefill of S one prompt at a time. It concluded that S must not prefill at all.
The second review corrected that ([05](05_limitations.md)).

