S holds {{ n(I.designs.s.area.resident_gb, 0) }} GB of weights, and a single token touches
{{ pct(W.touched_per_token.total / W.bytes.total_with_draft, 1) }} of them.

| S at batch 1 | system power | tokens/J | against G0 at batch 1 | against G0 at its best batch |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| idle banks in retention, idle lanes clock-gated | {{ n(I.power_management.managed.watts, -1) }} W | {{ n(I.power_management.managed.tokens_per_joule, 2) }} | {{ x(I.power_management.managed.over_g0_batch1) }} | {{ x(I.power_management.managed.over_g0_best_batch, 2) }} |
| everything at full voltage, clocks on | {{ n(I.power_management.unmanaged.watts, -1) }} W | {{ n(I.power_management.unmanaged.tokens_per_joule, 2) }} | {{ x(I.power_management.unmanaged.over_g0_batch1) }} | {{ x(I.power_management.unmanaged.over_g0_best_batch, 2) }} |

When it serves one request, S uses kilowatts to keep {{ I.designs.s.area.dies }} dies ready. With power management it
gets {{ x(I.power_management.managed.over_g0_best_batch, 2) }} of the tokens per joule of a GPU cluster that runs at its best batch.
It also serves that request {{ x(RS.speed_batch1) }} faster. Without power management, the figure is {{ x(I.power_management.unmanaged.over_g0_best_batch, 2) }}.
The efficient way to run S is busy (section 4).

The managed figure assumes a {{ n(S_HW.wake_latency * 1e9, 0) }} ns
wake from retention (assumption). No public source gives SRAM leakage at operating temperature at this node.
We sweep it across a 20x range (section 12).

